 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: Encoding is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, due to block-wise processing. Decoding is $O(1)$ per voxel during rendering, as it involves texture lookups and additions [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: The compressed size is $3 \times (N_x/4)(N_y/4)(N_z/4)$ bytes for the index texture, plus $256 \times 64 \times \text{sizeof(float)}$ for $C_1$ and $256 \times 8 \times \text{sizeof(float)}$ for $C_2$. This is a significant reduction from the original $N_x N_y N_z$ voxels [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity**: Encoding processes each voxel once in block-wise fashion [S1], yielding $O(N)$ time complexity [G]. Decoding performs $O(1)$ texture lookups and additions per voxel during rendering [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity**: The index texture occupies $3 \times (N_x/4)(N_y/4)(N_z/4)$ bytes [S1]. Codebooks $C_1$ and $C_2$ occupy $256 \times 64$ and $256 \times 8$ elements respectively [S1]. This yields a compressed size significantly smaller than the original $N_x N_y N_z$ voxels [G].
 *   **Measured Performance**: The method was tested on an ATI 9700 GPU [S1]. The use of dependent texture fetches allows for efficient on-chip decoding [S1].
 *   **Strengths**: High compression ratio, efficient GPU rendering, enhanced fidelity through separate quantization of bands [S1].
